  "notes": {
    "NJC-2025-P2-Q4": {
      "flaggedParts": [
        "(ii)"
      ],
      "flaggedAt": 1795500900000,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-njc-de-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(ii)",
          "tags": [
            "Forgot +c / Modulus",
            "Algebra Slip"
          ],
          "text": "Dropped the modulus signs when integrating $\\int -\\frac{k}{293 - 6.5h}\\,\\mathrm{d}h = \\frac{k}{6.5}\\ln|293 - 6.5h| + C$ and almost lost the $-$ sign from dividing by the coefficient of $h$. Also remember to explicitly state why $b = \\frac{k}{6.5} > 0$ since proportionality constant $k > 0$.",
          "updatedAt": "24/11/2026"
        },
        {
          "id": "demo-njc-de-2",
          "kind": "note",
          "part": "(iv)",
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Model limitation checklist: solve $293 - 6.5h = 0 \\implies h \\approx 45.1\\text{ km}$. For $h > 45.1$, pressure increases / temperature drops below $0\\text{ K}$, which is physically impossible.",
          "updatedAt": "24/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "HCI-2025-P1-Q9": {
      "flaggedParts": [
        "(b)"
      ],
      "flaggedAt": 1795489500000,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-hci-int-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(a)",
          "tags": [
            "Forgot +c / Modulus",
            "Careless / Arithmetic"
          ],
          "text": "Missed $+c$ on indefinite integral and flipped sign on the second integration by parts term: $\\int (2x - 1)\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x = (2x - 1)\\sin x + 2\\cos x + c$. Differentiate back quickly as a sanity check!",
          "updatedAt": "24/11/2026"
        },
        {
          "id": "demo-hci-int-2",
          "kind": "note",
          "part": "(b)",
          "tags": [],
          "text": "For $\\int_0^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} |2x - 1|\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, sketch/check sign change of $2x - 1$ inside $[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]$: negative on $[0, \\frac{1}{2}]$ and positive on $[\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]$, so split at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$ before reusing part (a).",
          "updatedAt": "24/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "RI-2025-P1-Q3": {
      "flaggedParts": [
        "(d)"
      ],
      "flaggedAt": 1795433400000,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-ri-vec-1",
          "kind": "note",
          "part": "(d)",
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Clean rhombus trick: since $|\\mathbf{a}| = |2\\mathbf{b}|$, the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{b}$ is a rhombus, so its diagonals $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular! Hence $\\text{Area} = |\\mathbf{a} \\times 2\\mathbf{b}| = \\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}||\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}|$.",
          "updatedAt": "23/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "NJC-2025-P2-Q10": {
      "flaggedParts": [
        "(v)"
      ],
      "flaggedAt": 1795356600000,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-njc-ht-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(v)",
          "tags": [
            "Misread Question",
            "GC Error"
          ],
          "text": "Two traps in one part: \"not to exceed $12.8$\" means claim $\\mu \\le 12.8$, so test upper-tailed $\\mathrm{H}_1: \\mu > 12.8$ at $2\\%$ (not 2-tailed!). Also entered variance $\\frac{0.25}{48}$ instead of $\\sqrt{\\frac{0.25}{48}}$ into GC `invNorm`.",
          "updatedAt": "22/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "DHS-2025-P2-Q4": {
      "flaggedParts": [
        "(c)"
      ],
      "flaggedAt": 1795351200000,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-dhs-vec-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(c)",
          "tags": [
            "Algebra Slip",
            "Accuracy (3sf / Exact)"
          ],
          "text": "Expanded $(3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot(3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b})$ carelessly as $-6(\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b})$ instead of $-12(\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b})$, and gave angle in radians when 1 d.p. in degrees ($104.5^\\circ$) is required.",
          "updatedAt": "22/11/2026"
        },
        {
          "id": "demo-dhs-vec-2",
          "kind": "note",
          "part": "(d)",
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Always check given magnitudes first: since $|\\mathbf{a}| = 1$, $\\mathbf{a}$ is already a unit vector, so $|\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}| = |\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf{a}}|$ is the length of projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$ (or line $OA$).",
          "updatedAt": "22/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "ASRJC-2026-P2-Q1": {
      "flaggedParts": [],
      "flaggedAt": null,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-asrjc-cn-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(Full) Find w",
          "tags": [
            "Conceptual Gap"
          ],
          "text": "Got $\\sqrt{5} + 2\\mathrm{i}$ instead of $-\\sqrt{5} + 2\\mathrm{i}$. Forgot to check which quadrant $w$ lies in: $\\arg(w) = 2.411865 \\in (\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi)$, so $w$ is in the 2nd quadrant ($x < 0, y > 0$).",
          "updatedAt": "21/11/2026"
        }
      ]
    },
    "NYJC-2025-P1-Q10": {
      "flaggedParts": [],
      "flaggedAt": null,
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "demo-nyjc-mac-1",
          "kind": "mistake",
          "part": "(b)",
          "tags": [
